---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CourierCountryFrameworks from "../../../components/CourierCountryFrameworks.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Courier framework — DelishAfrica®"
  description="Status, pay, data, safety and appeal: DelishAfrica Courier's public pre-launch framework."
  alternatePath="/courier/"
>
  <section class="gateway-v3 shell gateway-courier">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">COURIER · MOVE THE FUTURE</p>
      <h1>A clear mission. Readable rules. Human review.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">
        DelishAfrica is preparing Courier around a simple principle: no courier should discover their status,
        pay model, data use or appeal rights after they have started working.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="#courier-framework">Read the Courier framework</a>
        <a class="button button-ghost" href="/en/become-a-courier/">See the application journey</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="gateway-panel courier-panel">
      <span>COURIER FRAMEWORK · V0.1</span>
      <strong>TRUST<br/>IN MOTION</strong>
      <small>STATUS · PAY · DATA · APPEAL</small>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="courier-framework" class="section shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">BEFORE LAUNCH</p>
    <h2>Six design commitments before the first mission.</h2>
    <p class="hero-lead">
      This public framework is not yet an employment or commercial contract. Final conditions will be published
      country by country before any courier is activated.
    </p>

    <div class="courier-values">
      <article>
        <span>01 · STATUS</span>
        <h3>Reality before labels.</h3>
        <p>
          The applicable status will be determined by how the service actually operates and by local law, not by a
          contractual label alone. Related rights, obligations and conditions must be explained before activation.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span>02 · PAY</span>
        <h3>Understand before accepting.</h3>
        <p>
          The calculation method, known mission elements, payment schedule, fees and incentives must be readable.
          Any future rate card will be versioned and announced before it takes effect.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span>03 · MISSION CHOICE</span>
        <h3>No hidden penalty.</h3>
        <p>
          The product is designed to distinguish availability, offer and acceptance. Declining a mission should not,
          by itself, trigger automatic deactivation or lower access to future offers. Any constraint required for safety or local law must be stated.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span>04 · DEACTIVATION & APPEAL</span>
        <h3>Important decisions deserve a human.</h3>
        <p>
          A suspension or permanent deactivation should not rely solely on an automated decision. An understandable
          reason, a chance to present the courier's position and a human appeal channel are part of the target framework.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span>05 · DATA & ALGORITHMS</span>
        <h3>Location data needs a purpose.</h3>
        <p>
          Location, mission and performance data should be limited to what is necessary for the service, safety and
          applicable obligations. Automated systems that materially affect activity should be explained in plain language.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span>06 · SAFETY & PROTECTION</span>
        <h3>No mission without a protection framework.</h3>
        <p>
          Required insurance, incident handling, equipment, prevention and assistance will be defined by country before
          launch. DelishAfrica should not activate a market until mandatory protections have been checked.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell route-preview">
    <div class="route-map">
      <span class="route-point point-a">R</span>
      <span class="route-line"></span>
      <span class="route-point point-b">C</span>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">ETA &amp; MISSION CLARITY</p>
      <h2>Technology should support field decisions.</h2>
      <p>
        Distance, disclosed compensation, mission status and the next action should be understandable in seconds.
        Route intelligence is an operational aid, not a reason to hide the rules governing the work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CourierCountryFrameworks lang="en" />

  <section class="section shell policy-grid" id="dialogue">
    <article>
      <h2>Open today</h2>
      <p>
        Dialogue with courier representatives, legal-framework review, product preparation and Courier application testing.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h2>Not open yet</h2>
      <p>
        No paid missions, mass recruitment or public KYC-document collection are open from this landing until country
        conditions are finalized.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h2>One framework per country</h2>
      <p>
        Status, pay, insurance, taxation, appeal and required documents will be published through local annexes before
        activation in Belgium, France, Luxembourg or Cameroon.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell apply-bridge">
    <p class="eyebrow">FIELD CO-DESIGN</p>
    <h2>Build with couriers before building around them.</h2>
    <p>
      Feedback from couriers and their representatives is incorporated before operational launch. This framework will
      evolve as legal, contractual and product decisions are validated.
    </p>
    <a class="button button-primary" href="/en/become-a-courier/">Understand the future onboarding</a>
    <a class="text-link" href="/en/support/">Open support →</a>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
